### What the editor shows

An `.mdm` file is Markdown that VS Code opens as a document: prose, `code`,
equations in LaTeX and scores in ABC notation, each drawn where it is written.

A click on the score opens its ABC: `M:3/4` is the metre, `L:1/8` the unit
note length, `e` is `E` an octave up and `_e` is `e` flat.

```{.abc .play}
X:1
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=120
K:C
G3 E c2 | A4 F2 | [Ec]2 G2 E2 | G2 A2 B2 | [Ec]6 |]
```

The toolbar sets the theme, draws the staff lines in ink and lets `Ctrl+D`
find a selection inside other words, as `E` inside `[Ec]`. An equation sits
in the line, $f_{\texttt{A}} = 440\ \text{Hz}$, or on a line of its own:

$$
f_{\texttt{e}} = 2\, f_{\texttt{E}}, \qquad f_{\texttt{\_e}} = 2^{-1/12}\, f_{\texttt{e}}
$$

The headphones on a score open its player, and a playhead walks the staff
as the tune sounds.
